Pipeflow
--------

The ``pipeflow`` case is a very generic case that can readily be used as a
basis for many other projects. The domain is a tube (loaded from ``tube.stl``)
with periodicity in the x-direction. This variant is initialized with red
blood cells only, using RBC.pos.

The most important adjustable variables are in the ``config.xml`` file.

- ``<domain><refDirN>`` This controls how much fluid cells there are
  perpendicular to the flow direction, that is the same as the diameter of the
  pipe. One fluid cell usually translates to 0.5 µm in reality. Therefore, this
  parameter controls the size of your pipe!
- ``<domain><Re>`` Used to calculate the driving force of the periodic pipeflow
  system. The Reynolds number corresponds to the one that would be found when
  the system is run without any cells! Therefore, velocities might be a bit lower
  than expected with cells present. If you set this too high the simulation will
  become unstable!
- ``<domain><geometry>`` which stl file do you read in? The default is a simple
  periodic pipe.

The default ``*.pos`` file contains many positions for the red blood cells
generated by the ``packCells`` tool. When you increase the size of the pipe
more cells will fit but only according to these ``*.pos`` files. You might want
to create your own distributions with the following tool: :ref:`packcells`.


Local adhesion variant
----------------------

This directory was copied from src_hemocell/examples/pipeflow. Geometry,
RBC model and position files, flow driving, warmup, material/particle
update intervals, and time loop are unchanged. Old output and machine-specific
Slurm files were not copied. The CMake project and compile.sh now build this
root-level example against src_hemocell/build/libhemocell.a.

Build and run from this directory:

    ./compile.sh
    source ../src_hemocell/loadHemoCell.sh
    mpirun -np 2 ./pipeflow config.xml

The simulation explicitly uses a 2x1x1 block decomposition along x, with one
block per MPI rank. Exactly two ranks are required. The voxelized fluid/solid
mask is copied onto the same decomposition before initializing the lattice.

Cell-cell interactions use HemoCell::setAdhesion (short-range LJ repulsion,
Morse attraction, hard cutoff). The five physical parameters are copied from
bifurcation/config.xml:

    r0      = 0.2 um
    rc      = 1.0 um
    epsilon = 4.100531391e-22 J
    D0      = 4.100531391e-21 J
    alpha   = 3.0 um^-1

Only RBC is registered. Platelet registration, outputs, statistics and input
files have been removed. The existing adhesion API therefore acts only on
RBC-RBC pairs in this example.

Wall interactions use the official enableBoundaryParticles() /
applyBoundaryRepulsionForce() chain, with no wall adhesion. The wall formula
is F = kRep * (cutoff / r) * rhat for r < cutoff. The API converts cutoff
from um to LU but stores kRep directly as an LBM force coefficient; it is
neither an energy in joules nor a force in newtons.

The configured wall coefficient is 1e-3 LBM (matching bifurcation), with a
0.7 um cutoff. For dx=5e-7 m, dt=1e-7 s and rho=1025 kg/m^3:

    param::df = rho * dx^4 / dt^2 = 6.40625e-9 N per LBM force unit
    kRep * param::df = 6.40625e-12 N = 6.40625 pN

This is the coefficient, not a cap on force: each pair scales as cutoff/r
and multiple wall points contribute. The copied example's unused 2e-22
would be only 1.28125e-30 N as a force coefficient when passed directly to
this API. To specify a different physical coefficient, divide its value
in newtons by param::df before storing it in boundaryRepulsion/kRep.
The current value is a starting parameter, not a long-run stability result.
The official wall search and force formula are unchanged.

Both interaction terms update together at stepParticleEvery (5 by default).
The existing library clears the shared interaction slot, calculates cell-cell
adhesion, and then adds wall repulsion. OUTPUT_FORCE_REPULSION is enabled for
RBC and stores the combined interaction force. To investigate
time-step convergence, adjust the configured update intervals and dt; the
original material=20 and particle=5 intervals are preserved here.
